    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "994a87bc_a0416420",
        "filename": "authfs/aidl/com/android/virt/fs/IVirtFdService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-20T00:52:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s a blob and it doesn\u0027t matter.  The requester of the task (Android) has to trust the task service anyway, so it\u0027s ok to pass this information via binder.  The service treats the ID as a blob, since it\u0027s only used to refer back to the client, and doesn\u0027t need to trust the ID.",
      "parentUuid": "4c313ef5_b353a899",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 21,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 21,
        "endChar": 26
      },
      "revId": "15428a86ebeb078759c6609906043926417aaede",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "88665b72_e0dc6280",
        "filename": "authfs/aidl/com/android/virt/fs/IVirtFdService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-20T00:52:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The \"client\" here is the VM.  A service in the VM first receives ID from the request / task parameters, then set up the task to read/write the received ID/FD via authfs.  authfs here is the client of this interface.\n\nTo elaborate, Android is the role that delegate the task to the VM (via another binder service), so it is responsible to prepare the files/fds, then pass essential information (fds as numbers, which fd is for read/write, etc.) to the service in the VM.  Within the request, the service needs to configure authfs to handle the read/write requests.",
      "parentUuid": "1bbc1030_c9a0c3ee",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 21,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 21,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "15428a86ebeb078759c6609906043926417aaede",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fd116779_a105f417",
        "filename": "authfs/aidl/com/android/virt/fs/IVirtFdService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-23T00:28:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, that is my understanding, VM as client.\n\nSo then my questions are:\n- how do we obscure the FDs, so that they are per-client (maybe we want byte[] instead of int32_t for the FDs, but that could come later)\n- how do you get the size (you can\u0027t only pass the file size when you send the FD, since it may become quickly out of date, and interfaces wouldn\u0027t have more information. For instance, could you explain how fseek could work on top of this interface?)",
      "parentUuid": "88665b72_e0dc6280",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 21,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 21,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "15428a86ebeb078759c6609906043926417aaede",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d89c73a2_e36a2a0c",
        "filename": "authfs/aidl/com/android/virt/fs/IVirtFdService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-23T01:59:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This service will eventually be transient.  For example, for dex2oat to run in the VM, Android will need to start a fd_service and send the ID and it\u0027s address (TBD, still need to understand your remote binder API better) to the VM.  The task handler in the VM will then start the FUSE filesystem with parameter of backend address and file IDs.  So we won\u0027t need to worry about task accessing wrong files.\n\nThis change only adds read operation, and it\u0027s implicit here that the file is immutable. On the other side, Auth FS only implements fs-verity to verify file with *signature*. Since the VM does not trust others, it doesn\u0027t trust the information provided by this backend. Given a file\u0027s fs-verity signature (and a public key the VM can trust), Auth FS really only need is some \"data loader\" to fetch bytes from specified offset. If the data does not verify, the read must fail.\n\nRE fseek: since Android is not trusted, we wouldn\u0027t need to rely on the FD\u0027s state. We\u0027ll just always use pread (and pwrite later).\n\nLet me know if anything is unclear.",
      "parentUuid": "fd116779_a105f417",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 21,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 21,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "15428a86ebeb078759c6609906043926417aaede",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "337c993c_a0362b0e",
        "filename": "authfs/aidl/com/android/virt/fs/IVirtFdService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-23T02:25:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This service will eventually be transient\n\nThis isn\u0027t super easy/clean to do with servicemanagers, based on their name (assuming we\u0027ll use this). Usually people do something like this:\n\n    interface IManager {\n        ISession openSession();\n    }\n    interface ISession {\n        .. read();\n    }\n\nThat way, sessions can be dynamic, but you can still get ahold of a single service at the top (though I\u0027m not sure if you mean that the session should be associated with each FD send, in that case we could create and send the session when we marshall an FD).\n\n\u003e Auth FS really only need is some \"data loader\" to fetch bytes from specified offset.\n\nAck - I kind of missed this detail before. Though, I feel like the size might still be important, because for very large files, we would want to load data lazily. Maybe we just don\u0027t need it now?",
      "parentUuid": "d89c73a2_e36a2a0c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 21,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 21,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "15428a86ebeb078759c6609906043926417aaede",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "181e1410_7514cc9c",
        "filename": "authfs/aidl/com/android/virt/fs/IVirtFdService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-23T17:11:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do you have sample code that shows how remote binder works?  I\u0027ll take whatever you suggests to set up such a connection.\n\nConceptually, I\u0027m not sure `openSession` is needed since the transient service itself is kind of a short-lived session. Maybe some details can help: From previous security discussion, we don\u0027t want to change the existing SELinux policy for dex2oat. dex2oat is forked from installed, and does NOT have the privilege to open(2) any files.  That is, installd opens necessary files and passes the FDs to dex2oat.  To run dex2oat in the VM, we need this fd_server to serve the FDs, and fd_server doesn\u0027t need to maintain any state.  Also note that fd_server shouldn\u0027t be able to open arbitrary files on the filesystem, and is only supposed to expose files if appropriate.\n\n\u003e for very large files, we would want to load data lazily\n\nYes, that\u0027s the beauty of fs-verity :) Auth FS always load/verify data lazily. With the Merkle tree, fs-verity can verify the hash of the i-th 4K chunk matches the signed tree.  If i is out of bound, the read can simply fail.  The client of fd_service doesn\u0027t need to know the file size beforehand.\n\nLet me know if you want to talk about this over VC.",
      "parentUuid": "337c993c_a0362b0e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 21,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 21,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "15428a86ebeb078759c6609906043926417aaede",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
